  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 158 AM CDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\nThe heavy rain potential will continue through tonight, however the \nbiggest forecast change is that axis of heaviest precipitation \ncontinues to trend further south. This may prove helpful in \nmitigating flooding across northeast Kansas in northern Missouri \nwhere 1.5 up to 4 inches has already fallen and several area rivers, \ncreeks, and streams are forecast to rise, or are already in minor to \nmoderate flood. It is here an additional 0.50-1.50\" of precipitation \nis still expected to fall. The heavier rainfall amounts today \nthrough tonight are expected to fall along and south of the Missouri \nRiver where widespread 1-3\" of precipitation is forecast with \nlocally higher amounts possible. Despite the heavier precipitation \nfalling south of the current watch, an expansion is not anticipated \nat this time considering 3-hr flash flood guidance is 2.5 to 4 \ninches and 6-hour flash flood guidance is between 3-5 inches along \nand south of the Missouri River. As such widespread flooding is not \nexpected although localized flooding cannot be ruled out.\n\nToday, a closed upper level low over the southern High Plains this \nmorning will slowly move into the central Plains by this evening. \nSeveral lead shortwave will eject out ahead of this feature into the \nlocal area. This will bring the potential for several rounds of \nshowers and storms. An attendant cold front will slowly sink across \nthe forecast area and will be the focus for heavier precipitation. \nAdditional lift and moisture transport will also come courtesy of a \npersistent SSWly 35-40kt LLJ. All this, coupled with PWATs ranging \nin the 2.00\"-2.25\" range, which is in the 100th percentile range for \nearly October, will lead to very efficient rainfall. Tonight, the \nclosed low upper low will open up into a open wave over the eastern \nPlains and move through the local area tonight, this will bring one \nmore round of heavier rain the to area and force the cold front \nsoutheast of the CWA. Highs today will range from the upper 60s to \nmid 70s.\n\nBeyond tonight, the forecast looks mainly dry for the next week. \nHigh pressure will build into the area tomorrow behind the system \nthat exits the area tonight. This will provide cooler conditions \nwith highs in the 60s. The surface ridge of high pressure will \nremain in control on Saturday with highs in the mid 60s to lower \n70s. Sunday a upper level ridge will become more amplified over the \nsouthwestern CONUS. This will aid in providing subtle height rises \nover the local area allowing highs to rise into the 70s. Monday a \nsurface ridge of high pressure will build into the region from the \nCanadian Plains cooling temperatures back into the mid 60s to mid \n70s. A  warming trend is expected through the middle of next week as \nheight rises aid in temperatures rising back into the low 70s to \nlower 80s by Wednesday and Thursday.",
